  "slug": "spin-altermagnetism-d7e3",
  "summary": "New first-principles and symmetry analyses tie odd-parity magnetism and stacking or polarization order to measurable charge, spin, valley and thermal responses.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is theory that links measurable responses to symmetry-defined order. For odd-parity magnets, charge-spin selection rules and temporal-spectral separation are proposed as general transport signatures. In twisted bilayer NiPS3, a perpendicular electric field is predicted to reorient p-wave spin textures. Related first-principles work extends to polar and stacking order: shift current in hexagonal boron nitride multilayers tracks stacking and polarization, and sliding antiferroelectric four-layer MoS2 is predicted to host a quantum-metric-driven nonlinear valley Hall effect. Axial ferrons in uniaxial ferroelectrics are predicted to show thermal and angular-momentum Hall effects. What is new is the shared emphasis on electric-field or stacking tunability and on symmetry-based selection rules. All five items are preprints, and the predictions remain untested experimentally.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
